Z||000005||DXR_DAD||02-23-2000||07:38 PM||||William, any metal fabricator should be able to do the job, also some machine shops. A friend of mine that owns a antique metal restoration shop did mine, now he does them all the time. Its a small non-automotive shop.||||reg||1
Z||000006||William||02-23-2000||08:28 PM||||Looks like I might to the rims myself! I could not resist so I went out with a sanding block and sanded the center of one of the rims. WOW, all the corrosion and all the lathe lines were gone from the center in 2 minutes. This seems really easy! I was dreading striping the clear coat off the rims but get this, the sandpaper turned gray instantly. Apparently all the clearcoat has already faded away. This means I can refinish these rims with the tires still on. I can hardly wait for Saturday.||||reg||14
Z||000007||Monkeyman||02-24-2000||04:49 AM||||If the clearcoat's gone, don't sand them.  Get a good polisher and some jewelers rouge (stick form takes longer but is MUCH better).  Start with a fairly course grade (brown) and work your way up to the finest grade (white).  There are 5 different standard grades.  I don't know the grit #s, but the colors are:  brown, maroon, red, green, and white.  If there's a good size truck stop in your area, you can usually find brown, green and white there.||||reg||1
Z||000008||DXR_DAD||02-24-2000||06:00 PM||||William, there has to be a Grainger near you.<P>Brown  #3w769<BR>White  #3w770<BR>Red    #3w768<P>The red enhances luster to buffed metal<BR>is for precious metals. $4.59 a pack of 4 sticks.||||reg||1
Z||000009||William||02-24-2000||07:17 PM||||Monkeyman, I must sand the rims as the high tech style has small ridges caused by the lathe. These lines must be sanded off along with the corrsion. I'm not sure about using that colored polish stuff as I do not want a chrome look but rahter a bright shiney satin. after rough sanding I will go to 600 grit and determine what my next step will be at that time. I'll keep you guys posted with some pics.||||reg||1
